## D. Per-RTU groups vs one whole-model stream vs pull-bridges

800-tag model. `slice`: R multicast groups of K tags each (`publish_slices`); `whole`: one group of all 800 tags (every RTU would receive all of it);
`pull`: R real `ferrofire pull-bridge` processes with K tags each. The provider columns are the monitor's, over the 30 s window.

{{D}}

## E. Subscriber restart

The subscriber (multicast) leaves its group at t = 12 s and rejoins 2 s later with a fresh receiver; the pull-bridge process is killed at the same time and
respawned 2 s later (it re-validates its tags with a QUERY first). Time from the rejoin / respawn to the first complete cycle on the stream.
A multicast subscriber joins mid-stream and waits for the next full cycle, so its time is bounded by one period plus the cycle spread.

{{E}}

## F. Fault injection

### Provider killed (`kill -9`) and restarted 3 s later, publisher and REP both

Both streams ran side by side. The provider's `_ff.seq` restarts from 0 with a newer `_ff.t`; the receiver must not treat that as stale.

{{F_kill}}

### REP replies dropped for 4 s (tc: replies from port 5555 sent to a 100 % loss band)

The multicast path does not use that port. The pull-bridge times out (2 periods), recreates its socket and retries.

{{F_repdrop}}

### Garbage injected into the multicast group at 100 datagrams/s

Seven kinds in turn: noise, truncated (no NUL), another group, not UTF-8, an old replay, empty, cut mid-point. The control runs the same cell without injection.

{{F_inject}}

